public class ContactLookupAction {

    @InvocableMethod(label='Find Contact by Email' description='Looks up an existing Contact by email address (case-insensitive).')
    public static List<Result> find(List<Request> requests) {
        Integer size = requests.size();
        List<Result> results = new List<Result>(size);

        // Pre-normalize each request email: trim + lowercase.
        // Keep a parallel list so we can map results back in order.
        List<String> normalized = new List<String>(size);
        Set<String> uniqueEmails = new Set<String>();

        for (Integer i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            String raw = requests[i].email;
            if (raw != null) {
                raw = raw.trim();
            }
            if (raw != null && raw.length() > 0) {
                String lower = raw.toLowerCase();
                normalized.add(lower);
                uniqueEmails.add(lower);
            } else {
                normalized.add(null);
            }
        }

        // Single SOQL query – respects the running user's sharing.
        Map<String, Contact> emailToEarliest = new Map<String, Contact>();

        if (!uniqueEmails.isEmpty()) {
            List<Contact> contacts = [
                SELECT Id, Email, CreatedDate
                FROM Contact
                WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED
                WHERE Email IN :uniqueEmails
            ];

            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                String key = c.Email.toLowerCase();
                Contact existing = emailToEarliest.get(key);
                if (existing == null || c.CreatedDate < existing.CreatedDate) {
                    emailToEarliest.put(key, c);
                }
            }
        }

        // Build results in the same order as the input requests.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            Result r = new Result();
            String key = normalized.get(i);
            Contact c = (key != null) ? emailToEarliest.get(key) : null;
            if (c != null) {
                r.found = true;
                r.contactId = c.Id;
            } else {
                r.found = false;
                r.contactId = null;
            }
            results.add(r);
        }

        return results;
    }

    public class Request {
        @InvocableVariable(label='Email' required=true)
        public String email;
    }

    public class Result {
        @InvocableVariable(label='Contact Id')
        public Id contactId;

        @InvocableVariable(label='Found')
        public Boolean found;
    }
}
```

**Key design decisions:**

| Concern | How it's handled |
|---|---|
| **Case-insensitive matching** | The `Email` field on Contact is inherently case-insensitive in SOQL, and we additionally normalize both the input and the result key to lower-case so the in-memory `Map` lookup is deterministic. |
| **Trim / blank / null** | Each input is trimmed before use; if the result is empty after trimming, `found = false` with no query hit. |
| **One SOQL per call** | All valid (non-blank) emails are collected into a `Set` and issued as a single `WHERE Email IN :set` query, regardless of batch size. |
| **Earliest `CreatedDate`** | While iterating the query results we keep only the contact with the minimum `CreatedDate` per email key. |
| **Order preservation** | A parallel `List<String>` of normalized emails is walked in the same loop that builds `List<Result>`, guaranteeing one `Result` per `Request` in original order. |
| **Sharing** | `WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED` makes the sharing enforcement explicit (standard SOQL already enforces sharing, but the keyword documents intent and guards against future context changes). |
| **Bulk safety (200 rows)** | The pattern is O(n) with a single query; no per-record DML or SOQL, so it scales to the 200-row Flow batch limit without issue. |

