The task specifies `System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id))` — one job per case. However, the governor limit is **50 `System.enqueueJob` calls per transaction**. With the nightly cleanup closing up to 200 cases, a naïve one-job-per-case loop would throw `Too many enqueueJob calls` at case 51.

**Change made:** `CaseSurveyJob` accepts a `List<Id>` instead of a single `Id`. The trigger chunks eligible cases into batches of 50 and enqueues one job per chunk. Fault isolation is preserved *within* each job by wrapping every per-case `insert` in its own `try/catch`, so one bad record still cannot poison the other 49 in the same batch.

A defensive re-query inside `execute()` guards against the rare race where a case is reopened or its contact is removed between enqueue and execution.

