```apex
public class AccountCountrySync {

    /**
     * Bulk-safe handler for Account after-update.
     *
     * When an Account's BillingCountry changes, the MailingCountry of that
     * account's contacts is updated—*only* for contacts whose MailingCountry
     * is currently blank or still equal to the account's *previous*
     * BillingCountry.  Contacts working in a different country are untouched.
     *
     * Design notes
     * ────────────
     * • One SOQL query, scoped by AccountId to the set of accounts that
     *   actually changed country.  (An unbounded "SELECT … FROM Contact"
     *   would return 2.3 M rows and blow the 200 k-row bulk limit.)
     * • One DML statement (update) at the end.
     * • No SOQL/DML inside any loop.
     */
    public static void handleUpdate() {

        // ── 1. Detect which accounts actually changed BillingCountry ──────
        Map<Id, String> oldCountry = new Map<Id, String>();
        Map<Id, String> newCountry = new Map<Id, String>();

        for (Integer i = 0; i < Trigger.New.size(); i++) {
            String o = Trigger.Old[i].BillingCountry;
            String n = Trigger.New[i].BillingCountry;

            // Skip records where the field did not actually change
            // (guards against no-op updates and the edge case where
            //  old == new, which a DML "update" to the same value could
            //  theoretically produce).
            if (n != o) {
                oldCountry.put(Trigger.New[i].Id, o);
                newCountry.put(Trigger.New[i].Id, n);
            }
        }

        // Fast-exit: nothing to do
        if (oldCountry.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // ── 2. Single SOQL – contacts belonging to the affected accounts ──
        //     KeySet of the map is passed as the IN list so only relevant
        //     contacts are retrieved (typically a few hundred rows for a
        //     200-account batch, far under the 200 000-row bulk limit).
        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :oldCountry.keySet()
        ];

        // ── 3. Filter in memory ───────────────────────────────────────────
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            String prevCountry = oldCountry.get(c.AccountId);
            String currCountry = newCountry.get(c.AccountId);

            // Qualifies when MailingCountry is blank (null or empty string)
            // *or* still matches the account's previous BillingCountry.
            //
            // Apex string == is case-sensitive and treats null == null as
            // true, so both conditions are covered:
            //   • prevCountry is null  → c.MailingCountry == null matches
            //   • prevCountry is 'US'  → c.MailingCountry == 'US' matches
            //   • c.MailingCountry is null or '' → first two checks match
            Boolean isBlank = (c.MailingCountry == null || c.MailingCountry == '');
            Boolean matchesOld = (c.MailingCountry == prevCountry);

            if (isBlank || matchesOld) {
                c.MailingCountry = currCountry;
                toUpdate.add(c);
            }
        }

        // ── 4. Single DML ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

### Quick correctness checklist

| Scenario | Old `BillingCountry` | New `BillingCountry` | Contact `MailingCountry` | Result |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| HQ move, contact was local | `US` | `DE` | `US` | → `DE` |
| HQ move, contact was local (blank) | `US` | `DE` | *(null)* | → `DE` |
| HQ move, contact was local (empty str) | `US` | `DE` | `''` | → `DE` |
| HQ move, contact works abroad | `US` | `DE` | `FR` | **untouched** |
| Country unchanged | `US` | `US` | anything | **untouched** (skipped in step 1) |
| Country set for first time | *(null)* | `DE` | *(null)* | → `DE` |
| Country cleared | `US` | *(null)* | `US` | → *(null)* |

